Pass args during runtime:
  1)module_param(name,type,permission) ---> Macro used for declaring a single variable as a parameter for kernel module
        name ---> Name of variable/parameter used on cmd line
        type ---> Data type of parameter
        perm ---> Permissions for corresponding entry that is created in sysfs vfs (/sys/module/).
  2)module_param_array(name,type,num,perm) ---> Macro used to accept an entire array of values as a parameter.
        num ---> A pointer to an integer variable which shows number of elements passed by user.
  3)module_param_cb(name,ops,arg,perm) ---> Macro allows a module to register callback function that are executed when a parameter is read from or written to.
        ops ---> A pointer to a struct kernel_param_ops that contains set and get callback function pointers.
        arg ---> A pointer to a variable that holds parameter's actual value 
        
Major Number : It identifies the device driver in kernel that controls the class of devices. All devices controlled by same device driver share the same major number.

Minor Number : It is used by driver to distinguish between different devices controlled by same driver.

Allocation of Major and Minor Numbers :
   1)Static Allocation : This process is used when you want to reserve a specific, pre-determined major number.
   int register_chrdev_region(dev_t from, unsigned count, const char *name);    ---> Requests contiguous range of device numbers starting from dev_t value from.
          from ---> First device number . Created dev_t structure using MKDEV(int major, int minor)
          count ---> Total number of devices 
          name ---> Name of the device 
    2)Dynamic ALlocation : It is a process of allowing kernel to automatically assign a free major number at module load time.
    int alloc_chrdev_region(dev_t *dev, unsigned firstminor, unsigned count, const char *name);
          dev ---> Returns first allocated device number both major and firstminor in this pointer 
    3)New API for both static and dynamic allocation :
      int register_chrdev(unsigned int major, const char *name, const struct file_operations *fops);
      
Device File : It is a file-system object that serves as an interface to a device driver. They can be created manually as well as automatically. 
Manual Creation of device file:
      mknod -m <perm> <name> <device type> <major> <minor>
                0666   /dev/name   c/b
Automatic creation of device file(using udev):
   1)Allocate a device number.
        dev_t dev;
        alloc_chrdev_region(&dev,0,1,"mydev");
   2)Initialise and add dev.
        struct cdev my_cdev;
        cdev_init(&my_cdev,&fops);
        cdev_add(&my_cdev,dev,1);
   3)Create class
        struct class *cls;
        cls=class_create(struct module *owner,const char *name);
   4)Create device
        device_create(struct *class,struct device *parent, dev_t dev, void *drvdata,const char *fmt,....);
Inode : It is a fundamental data structure that stores all meta data about a file or directory, except for its name and actual content. It contains file type, permissions, ownership, timestamps, size, link count and data block pointers.

IOCTL():
   *The ioctl() (input/output control) command is a system call used to perform device specific operations that are not avaialble through standard file operations like read(), write().
   *It provides controlled communication between user applications and kernel space device driver.
   *Real time applications : Changing baud rate of serial port, adjusting volume, reading or writing device registers etc.
IOCTL() working:
   *Macros to define ioctl:
      __IO(type,nr) ---> No data transfer 
      __IOW(type,nr,data_type) ----> Write from user to driver
      __IOR(type,nr,data_type) ----> Read from driver to user 
      __IOWR(type_nr,data_type) ---> Both read and write
          type -----> MAGIC NUMBER 
          nr   -----> command
   *User-space calls:
       ioctl(fd, command, arg);
          fd --> File pointer
          command --> command number 
          arg --> argument/pointer to memory
   * Kernel space driver implements unlocked_ioctl, checks cmd, interprets arg and data is transferred using copy_to_user/ copy_from_user system calls.
       long(*unlocked_ioctl)(struct file *file, unsigned int cmd, unsigned long arg);
    * User Space                  Kernel Space
    
    ioctl(fd,SET,&val)-----> copy_from_user(&val,(int
    __user*)arg, sizeof(val))
    
    ioctl(fd,GET,&val)-----> copy_to_user((int __user *)arg, &val, sizeof(val))


copy_to_user/copy_from_user :
    *Kernel space and user space are seperated for protestion and a pointer in user space is not valid in kernel space. To safely transfer data between kernel space and user space, these are the helper functions.
    unsigned long copy_to_user(void __user *to,const void *from, unsigned long n); --------> copy data from kernel to user 
    unsigned long copy_from_user(const void *to,void __user *to, unsigned long n); --------> copy data from user to kernel 
